@title Dunmore Road intake: four ways to clear the weighbridge queue
@subtitle Yesterday's 16 arrivals replayed under four different fixes, ahead of September
@width wide

kpi Mean wait | 30.8 min | yesterday, 16 trucks | bad
kpi Longest wait | 58 min | Reyes-McAllister, 07:55 | bad
kpi Over 30 min | 9 of 16 trucks | arrivals 06:52 to 08:26 | bad
kpi Operators before 08:00 | 1 | 2 scales, so one sits idle | neutral

## The short version

First come, first served is not the problem by itself. The problem is that before 08:00 there is one operator for two scales, and the arrivals bunch up between 06:30 and 08:30. Four fixes are compared below. They attack four different things: capacity, when trucks arrive, how long each truck takes, and which trucks need the scale at all.

- **Second operator from 06:00** adds capacity. In the replay the mean wait falls from 32.8 min to 0.8 min. Budget: about $18,500 for the season.
- **Appointment slots** shape demand. They do not add capacity, so the same delay just moves off the yard and into the drivers' diaries.
- **Pre-registered fast lane** cuts handling time per truck. It works only if handling really drops, and nobody has measured that yet.
- **Gate moisture screen** removes work that never needed the scale. It helps, but only by a little on this log.

! The log has no date or day of week. This is one morning. If it was not a Tuesday to Thursday, scale the numbers up before you trust them for the rush you care about.

## What the log shows

data log = Arrival,Haulier,Load (bu),Crop,Moisture (%),Wait (min),Notes; 06:12,O'Brien Haulage,"1,240",Hard red wheat,13.8,4,; 06:20,Kowalczyk & Sons,980,Hard red wheat,14.1,11,"wanted lane 2; lane 2 shut for calibration"; 06:31,D'Angelo Farms,"2,115",Durum,13.2,19,"third trip today, “why do I wait with a full load?”"; 06:44,"Prairie Rose Co-op (Bin 7, east side)","3,480",Canola,8.9,27,; 06:52,Nguyen Bros,"1,050",Hard red wheat,15.6,31,"rejected: moisture over 14.5%, sent to dryer"
